  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, which involves analyzing how probabilities are distributed across outcomes, particularly favoring a higher concentration of probability on less bad outcomes with a small chance of a large loss (right-skew). The individual, however, focuses solely on the amount lost in the worst loss state, preferring the lottery where the loss is smallest in magnitude. This is a minimax loss criterion, not a skewness-based one. There is no indication that the individual considered the distribution shape, probability weighting, or asymmetry. The information used by the individual (size of loss) differs from that required by the reason (distribution of probabilities across outcomes). The justification (minimize loss amount) differs from the reason’s rule (prefer more right-skewed lottery). Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
